Research Experience____________________________________________________________
Center of Astrophysics | Harvard & Smithsonian (CfA) | SPARK Intern - Cambridge, MA 
September 2026 – Current 
Mentor: Dr. Rachel Bowens- Rubin (rbowru@umich.edu) 
• Developed a proposal for follow-up observation of the circumbinary system QY Aur, using JWST's MidInfrared Instrument (MIRI) to conduct proper-motion verification and assess the validity of multiple 
wide-orbit planetary-mass candidates. 
• Analysis of JWST MIRI direct-imaging data: processed MIRI Imager observational data of the 
circumbinary system QY Aur, implementing reference differential imaging (RDI) and principal 
component analysis (PCA) to suppress stellar flux to identify and characterize potential planetary 
companions. 
• Scientific paper in preparation: Currently writing a first-author research paper analyzing JWST MIRI 
direct-imaging observations of the circumbinary system QY Aur, detailing the methodologies for pointsource detection, extraction of mass and effective temperature from raw photometric measurements, 
and validating potential planetary-mass companions that would be the oldest known planets on P-type 
(circumbinary) orbits. 
Las Cumbres Observatory (LCO) | Science Intern- Goleta, CA
June – August 2026 
Advisors: Dr. Tim Lister (tlister@lco.global) & Dr. Carrie Holt (cholt@lco.global) 
• Coordinated and executed observations of the Didymos binary asteroid system with the 2-meter 
telescope at Siding Spring Observatory through the LCO Robotic Telescope Network, submitting 
observing requests and optimizing scheduling based on seeing conditions, lunar phase, and target 
visibility. 
• Implemented Differential Imaging Analysis (DIA) to extract and analyze light curves of the eclipsing 
binary using the NEOExchange Pipeline to constrain the orbital parameters in support of ESA’s followup mission to NASA’s DART planetary defense mission. 
• Gained hands-on experience with Django and developed a photometric data extraction Python 
wrapper to streamline astronomical data processing. 
• Performed photometric error analysis for quality control, determining telescope instrumentation issues 
and data reduction pipeline failures to reduce noise and improve light curve reliability. 

Mazin Lab | Undergraduate Research Intern - UC Santa Barbara 
April 2024 – June 2026 
PI: Dr. Ben Mazin (bmazin@ucsb.edu) 
Graduate Mentor: Nikki Zivkov (nzivkov@ucsb.edu) 
• Analyzed direct images of the K-dwarf, HIP 38514, obtained by SCExAO (Subaru Extreme Coronagraphic 
Extreme Adaptive Optics Telescope) and processed by MKID (Microwave Kinetic Inductance Detector) 
Pipeline. 
• Resolved the dynamical mass of the companion, HIP 38514 b, utilizing Orvara, an MCMC (Monte Carlo 
Markov Chain) orbital fitting program 
• Implemented SDI and ADI for PSF subtraction on images taken by CHARIS (Coronagraphic High Angular 
Resolution Imaging Spectrograph utilizing the pyKLIP library for direct image analysis of exoplanets. 
• Created and implemented python scripts to detect satellite spots in the CHARIS spectral images to aid 
in calibration and registration of the images in pyKlip 
• Extracted and examined the spectrum of HIP 38514-b and compared the data with known M and L 
stellar spectra. 
